A `std::promise` object uses its `set_value()` method to store a value that will be retrieved by its associated `std::future`.
```cpp
std::promise<int> p;
std::future<int> f = p.get_future();
// In a producer thread:
p.set_value(42);
// In a consumer thread:
int result = f.get(); // result is 42
```

The `std::promise` object can call its `set_exception()` method, passing an `std::exception_ptr`. When the consumer `std::future` calls `get()`, it will rethrow that exception.
```cpp
std::promise<int> p;
std::future<int> f = p.get_future();
// In a producer thread:
try {
    throw std::runtime_error("Operation failed!");
} catch (...) {
    p.set_exception(std::current_exception());
}
// In a consumer thread:
try {
    f.get(); // This will rethrow std::runtime_error
} catch (const std::exception& e) {
    // Handle exception
}
```

A "broken promise" occurs when a `std::promise` object is destroyed (e.g., goes out of scope) without having set a value or an exception. In this scenario, the associated `std::future` will throw a `std::future_error` exception with an error code of `std::future_errc::broken_promise` when its `get()` method is called. This indicates that the producer failed to fulfill its promise.
```cpp
std::future<int> f;
{ // Scope for promise
    std::promise<int> p;
    f = p.get_future();
    // p goes out of scope here without setting a value
}
try {
    f.get(); // Throws std::future_error with broken_promise
} catch (const std::future_error& e) {
    // e.code() == std::future_errc::broken_promise
}
```
